    "article": "**Why Your Sales Cycle Length Is Telling You More Than You Think**\n\nMost revenue operations teams track sales cycle length as a simple metric: how many days from first contact to closed deal. But when you look deeper, the sales cycle becomes one of the most diagnostic tools in your entire data stack.\n\nSales cycle analysis means breaking down the full journey a deal takes — from initial outreach, through discovery and proposal, all the way to signature. The goal is not just to measure time. The goal is to find where deals slow down, stall, or die.\n\nConsider a mid-market SaaS company selling a $50,000 annual contract. Their average sales cycle is 45 days. That number sounds clean and manageable. But when the RevOps team segments the data by deal source, a problem appears. Deals from inbound marketing close in 30 days. Deals from outbound prospecting take 68 days. Suddenly, that 45-day average is hiding a 38-day gap. That gap has real cost implications for forecasting, quota planning, and rep productivity.\n\nStage-level analysis makes the picture even clearer. When you calculate average time spent in each pipeline stage, patterns emerge. A deal might move quickly through discovery but slow dramatically at the proposal stage. This often signals a pricing objection, a missing stakeholder, or a competitor entering the conversation. Knowing this helps sales managers coach reps more effectively and helps RevOps adjust the process itself.\n\nAnother powerful technique is cohort analysis. Instead of averaging all deals together, you group deals by the month they entered the pipeline. Then you track how each cohort progresses over time. This method reveals seasonal patterns and shows whether process changes are actually improving cycle speed. For example, if you introduced a new proposal template in March, cohort analysis can tell you whether deals that started in March closed faster than those from February.\n\nDeal size also matters significantly. Enterprise deals almost always take longer than SMB deals, but many teams do not separate these when reporting cycle metrics. Mixing them produces misleading averages. Best practice is to segment by deal size, industry, and sometimes even by the individual account executive, since rep experience and behavior affect cycle length more than most leaders expect.\n\nFinally, cycle analysis should connect to revenue outcomes, not just speed. A shorter cycle is not automatically better. Some of the most profitable deals require longer nurturing. The real question is: which cycle patterns predict healthy, long-term customer relationships — and which ones predict churn?\n\nWhen RevOps teams treat the sales cycle as a diagnostic signal rather than a performance number, they uncover process gaps, coaching opportunities, and forecasting improvements that would otherwise stay invisible.",
    "writing_task": "**Your task:** Write **2–3 sentences** responding to this prompt:\n\n*What did you find most interesting in this article, and why?*\n\nTo guide you, focus on **one specific idea** from the article — for example, the hidden gap between inbound and outbound deal timelines, or the idea that a shorter sales cycle is not always better. Explain what surprised you or connected to your own work experience.\n\n**Sentence starters to help you (choose one or write your own):**\n- \"I found it interesting that...\"\n- \"The idea that... surprised me because...\"\n- \"In my work, I have seen something similar when...\"",
